#: ../../../devel/hexagon-l2vic.rst:4
msgid "Hexagon L2 Vectored Interrupt Controller"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../devel/hexagon-l2vic.rst:7
msgid ""
"            +-------------+                  +----------------------+\n"
"            |    l2vic    |                  |     hexagon core     |\n"
"            |             |                  |                      |\n"
"IRQ in ---->|             |                  |                      |\n"
"IRQ in ---->|    VID0    -|----------------->| irq2                 |\n"
"  ...  ---->|             |                  |   |                  |\n"
"IRQ in ---->|             |                  |   v                  |\n"
"            |     ...     |                  | <int steering>       |\n"
"            |             |                  |  /   |    |    \\     |\n"
"IRQ in ---->|             |                  | t0   t1   t2   t3 ...|\n"
"IRQ in ---->|    VIDN    -|                  |                      |\n"
"  ...  ---->|             |                  |                      |\n"
"IRQ in ---->|             |                  | Global SREG File     |\n"
"            |             |                  |                      |\n"
"            |    State    |                  |                      |\n"
"            | [      ] <--|==================|==> [ VID  ]          |\n"
"            | [      ] <--|==================|==> [ VID1 ]          |\n"
"            |             |                  |                      |\n"
"            +-------------+                  +----------------------+"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../devel/hexagon-l2vic.rst:30
msgid "L2VIC/Core Integration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../devel/hexagon-l2vic.rst:32
msgid "hexagon core supports 8 external interrupt sources"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../devel/hexagon-l2vic.rst:33
msgid "l2vic supports 1024 input interrupts mapped among 4 output interrupts"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../devel/hexagon-l2vic.rst:34
msgid "l2vic has four output signals: { VID0, VID1, VID2, VID3 }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../devel/hexagon-l2vic.rst:35
msgid ""
"l2vic device has a bank of registers per-VID that can be used to query the "
"status or assert new interrupts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../devel/hexagon-l2vic.rst:37
msgid ""
"Interrupts are 'steered' to threads based on { thread priority, 'EX' state, "
"thread interrupt mask, thread interrupt enable, global interrupt enable, "
"etc. }."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../devel/hexagon-l2vic.rst:40
msgid ""
"Any hardware thread could conceivably handle any input interrupt, dependent "
"on state."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../devel/hexagon-l2vic.rst:42
msgid ""
"The system register transfer instruction can read the VID0-VID3 values from "
"the l2vic when reading from hexagon core system registers \"VID\" and "
"\"VID1\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../devel/hexagon-l2vic.rst:44
msgid ""
"When l2vic VID0 has multiple active interrupts, it pulses the VID0 output "
"IRQ and stores the IRQ number for the VID0 register field.  Only after this "
"interrupt is cleared can the l2vic pulse the VID0 output IRQ again and "
"provide the next interrupt number on the VID0 register."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../devel/hexagon-l2vic.rst:48
msgid ""
"The ``ciad`` instruction clears the l2vic input interrupt and un-disables "
"the core interrupt.  If some/an l2vic VID0 interrupt is pending when this "
"occurs, the next interrupt should fire and any subsequent reads of the VID "
"register should reflect the newly raised interrupt."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../devel/hexagon-l2vic.rst:52
msgid ""
"In QEMU, on an external interrupt or an unmasked-pending interrupt, all "
"vCPUs are triggered (has_work==true) and each will grab the IO lock while "
"considering the steering logic to determine whether they're the thread that "
"must handle the interrupt."
msgstr ""
